  "judge_reasoning": "## Evaluation\n\n**Task Instruction:** Determine the convergence or divergence of the series Σ (n=1 to ∞) of 1/(n³ + 1).\n\n**Analysis of Screenshots:**\n\n1. **Screenshot 1:** Wolfram Alpha homepage with an empty input field — initial state.\n\n2. **Screenshot 2:** The query \"sum 1/(n^3+1), n=1 to infinity\" has been entered into the Wolfram Alpha search box, correctly representing the series from the instruction.\n\n3. **Screenshot 3:** Results begin loading, showing an \"Alternate form\" of the sum expressed via the digamma function, confirming the query was submitted.\n\n4. **Screenshots 4 & 5:** The full results page displays:\n   - **Infinite sum:** Σ (n=1 to ∞) 1/(n³+1) ≈ 0.68650 + 0.×10⁻²² i\n   - **Sum convergence:** Σ (n=1 to ∞) 1/(n³+1) **converges**\n   - Partial sum formula and a partial sums graph (which visibly plateaus, consistent with convergence)\n\n**Comparison with Result Response:**\n\nThe response states the series converges and cites the Wolfram Alpha \"Sum convergence\" result showing \"converges\" with an infinite sum ≈ 0.68650. This matches exactly what is visible in the screenshots:\n- The \"Sum convergence\" pod explicitly displays \"converges\" ✓\n- The infinite sum value ≈ 0.68650 is shown ✓\n\nThe mathematical conclusion is also correct — since 1/(n³+1) < 1/n³ and Σ 1/n³ is a convergent p-series (p = 3 > 1), the comparison test confirms convergence.\n\n**Conclusion:** The query was properly formulated, submitted, and the answer (convergence) was both obtained from the authoritative source and correctly reported, matching the screenshot evidence.\n\n## Verdict: **SUCCESS**",
